      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Whatever the exact details regarding feasibility or implementation might be (since I'm aware this might be difficult), this is definitely something worth thinking about in general. AV3 offers so much ability to do cool things whilst still allowing for good optimisation, but right now doing just about anything with it is somewhat punished by the performance ratings.\nNaturally there's a balance to be found between not cramming too much into any single avatar, versus not forcing people to load multiple separate avatars in series.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2020-11-13T14:11:26.252Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "I myself and quite a few people in my friends group love using avatar 3.0 and i see a lot of people are now condensing multiple avatars into one, with the use of the gesture wheel and changing outfits that way instead of changing to a different avatar for a different outfit. \nBut condensing outfits in this way can lead to the poly limit/mesh limit being exceeded. As outfits (in most cases) wouldn't be activated at the same time, would there be a way maybe of actively tracking enabled meshes and the polys on the active meshes and calculating the avatar performance on that instead?\nI don't know what performance difference there is between active and inactive meshes, but changing the system to something like this would allow a lot more avatars to be in the medium and good range and be visible for people to see rather than blocked due to being very poor from poly count.",
